1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,679
In 1999, someone took a picture of a
Bulbasaur… inside a video game.

2
00:00:04,729 --> 00:00:06,880
Not a professional photographer.

3
00:00:06,930 --> 00:00:12,591
Just a Pokémon Snap player who sent their best
shot to a Japanese magazine contest.

4
00:00:13,083 --> 00:00:16,653
In August 2026, that photo,
turned into a trading card,

5
00:00:16,703 --> 00:00:22,050
sold at auction for eight hundred nine
thousand, nine hundred forty-three dollars.

6
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:23,959
For a picture of a Bulbasaur.

7
00:00:24,009 --> 00:00:27,184
It's the most expensive
Bulbasaur card ever sold…

8
00:00:27,234 --> 00:00:31,660
and by the end of this video, you'll
know exactly how that happened.

9
00:00:31,710 --> 00:00:36,596
But first, let me give the number one
Pokémon the introduction it deserves.

10
00:00:37,083 --> 00:00:39,679
Number 001 in the National Pokédex.

11
00:00:39,729 --> 00:00:41,796
Bulbasaur, the Seed Pokémon.

12
00:00:41,846 --> 00:00:43,459
Grass and Poison type.

13
00:00:43,509 --> 00:00:47,994
It stands about two foot four,
weighs around fifteen pounds,

14
00:00:48,044 --> 00:00:52,681
and evolves into Ivysaur at level 16
and Venusaur at level 32.

15
00:00:53,183 --> 00:00:56,820
It debuted in 1996 with Pokémon
Red and Green in Japan,

16
00:00:56,870 --> 00:00:59,166
as one of the three Kanto starters.

17
00:00:59,216 --> 00:01:01,646
And here's a detail most people miss:

18
00:01:01,696 --> 00:01:03,724
of the three original starters,

19
00:01:03,774 --> 00:01:07,344
it's the only one with two types
right from the start.

20
00:01:07,850 --> 00:01:10,288
This year, Pokémon turns thirty.

21
00:01:10,338 --> 00:01:14,252
And in every Pokédex, every
list, every collection,

22
00:01:14,302 --> 00:01:17,518
page one has always belonged to Bulbasaur.

23
00:01:18,017 --> 00:01:22,440
According to the Pokédex, it's born with
a rare seed planted on its back,

24
00:01:22,490 --> 00:01:24,585
and that plant grows along with it.

25
00:01:24,635 --> 00:01:26,608
It also loves napping in the sun,

26
00:01:26,658 --> 00:01:30,407
because that's how the seed soaks up
sunlight and gets bigger.

27
00:01:30,457 --> 00:01:33,594
That's why you'll so often find
it lying in the sun:

28
00:01:33,644 --> 00:01:35,678
for Bulbasaur, resting is growing.

29
00:01:36,183 --> 00:01:38,412
So who came up with this little guy?

30
00:01:38,462 --> 00:01:43,665
Bulbasaur was designed by Atsuko Nishida, one
of the artists on the original games,

31
00:01:43,715 --> 00:01:47,209
and finalized by Ken Sugimori,
the series' art director.

32
00:01:47,717 --> 00:01:49,317
And here's the fun part:

33
00:01:49,367 --> 00:01:51,997
Nishida said she designed it backwards.

34
00:01:52,047 --> 00:01:54,885
Venusaur, the final evolution, came first,

35
00:01:54,935 --> 00:01:58,047
and she worked her way back
to the little one.

36
00:01:58,097 --> 00:02:00,384
So Bulbasaur was technically born…

37
00:02:00,434 --> 00:02:02,034
after its own evolution.

38
00:02:02,550 --> 00:02:07,229
And the inspiration? Sugimori
confirmed it: onions and bullfrogs.

39
00:02:07,279 --> 00:02:09,848
Once you see it, you can't unsee it.

40
00:02:09,898 --> 00:02:14,068
The chubby body, the wide mouth,
the spots… that's a frog.

41
00:02:14,118 --> 00:02:19,088
And the thing on its back looks a lot
more like an onion than a seed.

42
00:02:19,583 --> 00:02:22,533
In Japanese, it's called Fushigidane.

43
00:02:22,583 --> 00:02:29,182
"Fushigi" means mysterious or strange, and
"tane" means seed: the mysterious seed.

44
00:02:29,232 --> 00:02:32,020
It's also a pun on "fushigi da ne",

45
00:02:32,070 --> 00:02:37,372
which roughly means "isn't that
strange?" In English, it's simple:

46
00:02:37,422 --> 00:02:39,804
bulb plus dinosaur. Bulbasaur.

47
00:02:40,317 --> 00:02:44,597
And this is my favorite part. Is
Bulbasaur a plant… or an animal?

48
00:02:44,647 --> 00:02:49,326
Sounds like a joke, but it keeps coming
up in the Pokémon world itself:

49
00:02:49,376 --> 00:02:52,990
even the researchers can't agree
on how to classify it.

50
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:58,453
Thirty years later, Pokémon number one still
doesn't have an answer. And honestly?

51
00:02:58,503 --> 00:03:03,516
I love that. The very first entry in the
Pokédex was a mystery from day one.

52
00:03:04,017 --> 00:03:07,135
In the games, picking
Bulbasaur was the smart move.

53
00:03:07,185 --> 00:03:10,737
Its Grass-type attacks hit the
first two Gym Leaders hard:

54
00:03:10,787 --> 00:03:14,216
Brock and his Rock types, and
Misty and her Water types.

55
00:03:14,266 --> 00:03:17,880
Starting your journey with
Bulbasaur was way more relaxing.

56
00:03:17,930 --> 00:03:21,235
If you ever struggled against
Brock with a Charmander…

57
00:03:21,285 --> 00:03:23,036
now you know what you missed.

58
00:03:23,550 --> 00:03:26,453
In Pokémon Yellow, you
didn't get to choose:

59
00:03:26,503 --> 00:03:28,668
Pikachu was your starter, period.

60
00:03:28,718 --> 00:03:31,151
But there was a way to get Bulbasaur.

61
00:03:31,201 --> 00:03:34,305
In Cerulean City, Melanie
would give it to you…

62
00:03:34,355 --> 00:03:37,660
but only if your Pikachu
really, really liked you.

63
00:03:37,710 --> 00:03:41,821
Take good care of Pikachu, and
Bulbasaur would join your team.

64
00:03:41,871 --> 00:03:44,439
In Let's Go, same idea, different test:

65
00:03:44,489 --> 00:03:48,264
you had to have caught at least
thirty different species.

66
00:03:48,750 --> 00:03:52,826
And in 2016, Bulbasaur was one of
the starters in Pokémon GO.

67
00:03:52,876 --> 00:03:57,086
For a lot of people, it was the
first Pokémon they ever caught…

68
00:03:57,136 --> 00:03:58,574
out in the real world.

69
00:03:59,083 --> 00:04:03,624
But if there's one Bulbasaur that won over
an entire generation, it's Ash's.

70
00:04:03,674 --> 00:04:06,101
It showed up in episode ten of the anime.

71
00:04:06,151 --> 00:04:08,940
It lived in a hidden village
deep in the woods,

72
00:04:08,990 --> 00:04:13,169
where a girl named Melanie looked after
abandoned and injured Pokémon,

73
00:04:13,219 --> 00:04:15,646
and Bulbasaur was the village's guardian.

74
00:04:15,696 --> 00:04:18,243
At first, it wanted
nothing to do with Ash.

75
00:04:18,293 --> 00:04:22,230
But when Ash risked everything to save
an Oddish from Team Rocket,

76
00:04:22,280 --> 00:04:23,861
Bulbasaur changed its mind.

77
00:04:23,911 --> 00:04:26,640
And it was Melanie who told
it to go with him,

78
00:04:26,690 --> 00:04:29,962
because it needed to leave the
village to keep growing.

79
00:04:30,450 --> 00:04:34,508
Then came one of the moments that
defines this Bulbasaur:

80
00:04:34,558 --> 00:04:37,680
it had the chance to
evolve… and it said no.

81
00:04:37,730 --> 00:04:40,274
It chose to stay exactly who it was.

82
00:04:40,783 --> 00:04:44,877
Later on, Professor Oak asked Ash to
leave Bulbasaur at his lab,

83
00:04:44,927 --> 00:04:49,538
because the Pokémon there kept fighting
and nobody could keep the peace.

84
00:04:49,588 --> 00:04:53,422
Bulbasaur stayed… and became the
lab's official peacekeeper.

85
00:04:53,472 --> 00:04:57,889
At one point it even uses Dig, a move
Bulbasaur normally can't learn,

86
00:04:57,939 --> 00:05:02,162
and Oak explains it pulled it off
because it was a true emergency.

87
00:05:02,212 --> 00:05:05,981
And yes, it even had a little
romance with May's Bulbasaur.

88
00:05:06,031 --> 00:05:09,024
Turns out even the
peacekeeper has a soft side.

89
00:05:09,517 --> 00:05:12,159
So how do fans feel about it today?

90
00:05:12,209 --> 00:05:18,314
In 2020, Pokémon teamed up with Google for an
official Pokémon of the Year vote.

91
00:05:18,364 --> 00:05:22,084
Among the Kanto Pokémon,
Bulbasaur came in third.

92
00:05:22,134 --> 00:05:26,315
Behind Charizard and Gengar… and
ahead of Pikachu. Yep.

93
00:05:26,365 --> 00:05:30,469
The face of the entire
franchise, beaten by Bulbasaur.

94
00:05:30,983 --> 00:05:33,684
I think it comes down to
what it represents.

95
00:05:33,734 --> 00:05:35,747
Bulbasaur is where it all begins:

96
00:05:35,797 --> 00:05:40,185
page one of the Pokédex, the starter
that made your first steps easier,

97
00:05:40,235 --> 00:05:42,748
the loyal Pokémon that refused to change.

98
00:05:42,798 --> 00:05:45,249
It's not the flashiest or the strongest.

99
00:05:45,299 --> 00:05:50,813
But it's the kind of Pokémon that reminds you why
you started playing in the first place.

100
00:05:51,317 --> 00:05:55,161
And now, the part a lot of you have
been waiting for: the cards.

101
00:05:55,211 --> 00:05:58,265
Because for a Pokémon with
such a chill reputation,

102
00:05:58,315 --> 00:06:02,343
Bulbasaur is hiding some of the most
surprising cards in the hobby.

103
00:06:02,850 --> 00:06:05,206
Let's start at the very beginning.

104
00:06:05,256 --> 00:06:09,735
The first Bulbasaur card ever printed
came out in Japan in 1996,

105
00:06:09,785 --> 00:06:11,787
in the game's very first set.

106
00:06:11,837 --> 00:06:14,760
That first print run had no rarity symbol,

107
00:06:14,810 --> 00:06:18,015
so collectors call it the
"No Rarity" version.

108
00:06:18,065 --> 00:06:20,209
In perfect condition, a PSA 10,

109
00:06:20,259 --> 00:06:23,959
it goes for around three and a
half thousand dollars.

110
00:06:24,450 --> 00:06:29,264
The first English Bulbasaur came in the
1999 Base Set, card number 44.

111
00:06:29,314 --> 00:06:32,251
And this shows just how
wild this hobby is:

112
00:06:32,301 --> 00:06:35,656
the regular version is worth
about three dollars.

113
00:06:35,706 --> 00:06:40,728
But the exact same card, with the first
edition stamp and a PSA 10 grade,

114
00:06:40,778 --> 00:06:43,854
goes for around eight
thousand seven hundred.

115
00:06:43,904 --> 00:06:45,869
Same artwork, same Bulbasaur…

116
00:06:45,919 --> 00:06:47,884
thousands of times the price.

117
00:06:48,383 --> 00:06:50,525
But this one's the real surprise:

118
00:06:50,575 --> 00:06:53,182
the 2002 Legendary Collection Bulbasaur.

119
00:06:53,232 --> 00:06:56,570
It was a common, the kind
you'd pull from any pack.

120
00:06:56,620 --> 00:07:00,423
But its reverse holo version, with
that fireworks pattern,

121
00:07:00,473 --> 00:07:03,611
is incredibly hard to find in
perfect condition.

122
00:07:03,661 --> 00:07:08,394
And in PSA 10, it's worth around sixteen
thousand seven hundred dollars.

123
00:07:08,444 --> 00:07:09,257
For a common.

124
00:07:09,750 --> 00:07:13,459
Then there are the Japanese
promos, like Erika's Bulbasaur,

125
00:07:13,509 --> 00:07:15,498
given away by CoroCoro magazine,

126
00:07:15,548 --> 00:07:20,341
which goes for around eight and a half
thousand dollars in PSA 10. And then…

127
00:07:20,391 --> 00:07:23,336
there's the card I told you
about at the start.

128
00:07:23,850 --> 00:07:27,692
In 1999, Pokémon Snap came out in
Japan for the Nintendo 64:

129
00:07:27,742 --> 00:07:31,714
a game where you didn't battle
Pokémon, you photographed them.

130
00:07:31,764 --> 00:07:36,320
CoroCoro magazine ran a contest where
players sent in their best shots,

131
00:07:36,370 --> 00:07:40,147
and the winning photos were turned
into real trading cards.

132
00:07:40,197 --> 00:07:43,845
Cards that only the winners
received, in tiny print runs.

133
00:07:43,895 --> 00:07:45,791
Just a handful of copies each.

134
00:07:45,841 --> 00:07:49,034
And the Bulbasaur was card
number one in that set.

135
00:07:49,550 --> 00:07:54,685
In November 2023, a PSA 9 copy sold for
about eighty thousand dollars.

136
00:07:54,735 --> 00:07:58,982
In July 2025, another PSA 9 went
for two hundred thousand.

137
00:07:59,032 --> 00:08:03,500
And in August 2026, at Goldin's
summer auction, a PSA 8 copy,

138
00:08:03,550 --> 00:08:08,019
not even the top grade, sold for
eight hundred nine thousand,

139
00:08:08,069 --> 00:08:12,093
nine hundred forty-three dollars
after forty-nine bids.

140
00:08:12,143 --> 00:08:15,501
It's the all-time record for
a Bulbasaur card,

141
00:08:15,551 --> 00:08:20,168
more than six hundred thousand
dollars above the previous high.

142
00:08:20,683 --> 00:08:22,900
Think about that for a second:

143
00:08:22,950 --> 00:08:27,584
a photo someone took while playing a
Nintendo 64 back in 1999…

144
00:08:27,634 --> 00:08:30,531
is now worth more than a lot of houses.

145
00:08:30,581 --> 00:08:32,722
And it's not the only oddity:

146
00:08:32,772 --> 00:08:38,691
a 2000 Topps Chrome Bulbasaur in PSA 10 sold
in October 2026 for fifty thousand

147
00:08:38,741 --> 00:08:40,277
four hundred dollars.

148
00:08:40,783 --> 00:08:45,493
So next time you open a Pokédex and
see number 001, remember this.

149
00:08:45,543 --> 00:08:51,045
Behind that small, quiet Pokémon there's a
designer who created it backwards,

150
00:08:51,095 --> 00:08:56,814
a mystery nobody has solved in thirty years,
a Bulbasaur that refused to evolve…

151
00:08:56,864 --> 00:09:01,501
and a video game photo worth over
eight hundred thousand dollars.

152
00:09:02,017 --> 00:09:05,148
In the next chronicle, we're
taking on its evolution:

153
00:09:05,198 --> 00:09:08,150
Ivysaur, the middle child that
nobody talks about.

154
00:09:08,200 --> 00:09:10,551
If you don't want to miss it, subscribe.

155
00:09:10,601 --> 00:09:12,472
And let me know in the comments:

156
00:09:12,522 --> 00:09:13,852
did you pick Bulbasaur?
